# Feature Audit: Integrations and Provider Secrets

## 1. Scope

This audit covers provider credentials, secret storage, secret references, integration health, OAuth/delegated authorization, Tool Gateway upstream auth, SDK/adapters, and third-party tool authorization. It inspects `product_platform/integrations`, `tool_gateway`, provider credential migrations, integration routes in `api/app.py`, Agent OS OpenAI adapters, and `ophanix-python-sdk`. Relevant benchmarks are AWS AgentCore Identity, Arcade authorization challenge, Composio managed OAuth/tool execution, OpenAI Agents SDK approval/MCP integrations, and enterprise secrets/BYOK patterns. Out of scope: generic API keys and human auth, covered in AUTH.

## 2. Current Implementation Summary

Provider credentials are stored through `0036_provider_credentials.up.sql` and repository code in `product_platform/integrations/repository.py`. Secret providers live in `integrations/secrets.py`, with in-memory and environment-variable-backed options. Health checks live in `integrations/health.py`. Integration routes appear around `api/app.py:11140` and `api/app.py:11178`. Tool Gateway upstream auth modes are modeled in `tool_gateway/models.py` and executed through Tool Gateway invocation. The Python SDK uses static/env gateway bearer token providers. Agent OS includes OpenAI integration/adapters that can record tool arguments in context.

Current workflow: users can create/list provider credentials, use secret refs, run synthetic health checks, define upstream targets with API-key/bearer auth, and call tools through the gateway. There is no first-class OAuth authorization challenge, delegated user credential lifecycle, vault/KMS integration, or independent secret access audit.

